### 4.1 Project Management Process (PM)

#### 4.1.1 Purpose

The purpose of the Project Management Process is to establish and carry out the systematic way of the task of the software implementation project, which allows complying with the project's objectives in the expected quality, time and costs.

#### 4.1.2 Project Management Activities

The following activities are performed in accordance with ISO/IEC 29110:

**PM.01 – Project Planning**

The project plan for the execution of the project is developed according to the Statement of Work (Proposal) and validated with the customer (Project Advisor), where tasks and resources necessary to complete the work are sized and estimated. This Project Plan document (`BridgeU-projectplan`) serves as the baseline project plan, defining project scope, deliverables, and milestones (Section 1, 2, 6), detailed Work Breakdown Structure (Section 7) with task breakdown for each phase, resource requirements (Section 8) including tools, technologies, and human resources, risk management strategy (Section 9) with identified risks and mitigation plans, and schedule with specific dates for each phase (Phase 1-4). The project plan is reviewed and approved by the advisor before execution begins.

**PM.02 – Project Monitoring, Control, Risk Management, and Closure**

Progress of the project is monitored against the Project Plan and recorded in the Progress Status Record, with weekly progress updates provided to the advisor documenting completed tasks from the WBS (Section 7), current work in progress with status indicators, blockers or issues encountered and their resolution plans, and next week's planned activities aligned with the schedule. Corrections to remediate problems and deviations from the plan are taken when project targets are not achieved, including schedule adjustments when tasks take longer than estimated, resource reallocation when needed, and scope adjustments (if approved through change management). Appropriate treatment is taken to correct or avoid the impact of risks (see Section 9 for detailed risk management), including risk monitoring and review at regular intervals, implementation of mitigation strategies when risks materialize, and update of risk register with current status. Closure of the project is performed to get the customer acceptance documented in the acceptance record, including final presentation and demo to advisor and committee, advisor approval and acceptance record, and project archive and submission of all deliverables.

**PM.03 – Change Management**

Change Requests are addressed through their reception and analysis, where changes to software requirements are evaluated for cost, schedule and technical impact including impact assessment on scope, schedule, resources, and deliverables, technical feasibility analysis, and effort estimation for implementing the change. The change request process (detailed in Section 13) involves: (1) identifying and documenting proposed change with rationale, (2) assessing impact on scope, schedule, resources, and deliverables, (3) reviewing with advisor for approval/rejection, (4) if approved, updating relevant documents (SRS, SDD, Project Plan, Test Plan), (5) updating Traceability Record if requirements change, and (6) re-baselining affected work products. All change requests are tracked and documented for audit purposes.

**PM.04 – Communication and Agreement Tracking**

Review meetings with the Work Team and the Customer (Advisor) are held, including regular weekly consultations to discuss progress and issues, milestone review meetings (Proposal defense, Progress I, Progress II, Final presentation), and ad-hoc meetings when critical issues arise. Agreements are registered and tracked, with meeting minutes documenting decisions and action items, follow-up on action items in subsequent meetings, formal approval of deliverables documented in milestone reviews, and action item tracking until completion. Communication channels are established, including email for formal communications and document sharing, regular meeting schedule for synchronous discussions, and project repository for document access.

**PM.05 – Risk Identification**

Risks are identified as they develop and during the conduct of the project, with a risk register maintained (Section 9) containing risk description, probability, impact, and severity rating, mitigation strategies and contingency plans, risk owner and monitoring schedule, and current status and review dates. New risks are added to the register as they are identified during project execution, including technical risks (e.g., API integration challenges, performance issues), schedule risks (e.g., task delays, resource unavailability), and scope risks (e.g., requirement changes, feature creep). Risk review is conducted regularly (at least monthly) to assess current risk status.

**PM.06 – Software Version Control and Configuration Management**

A software version control strategy is developed (Section 5.2), including Git repository for source code and Markdown documentation, meaningful commit messages for each change following conventions, branch strategy for experimental features (feature branches merged to main after testing), and tag releases for each milestone (v1.0, v2.0, etc.). Items of software configuration are identified, defined and baselined, including source code (frontend Vue.js and backend Spring Boot), database schema and Flyway migration scripts, documentation (Proposal, SRS, SDD, Test Plan, Project Plan, Final Report), build scripts (Maven/Gradle, npm package.json), and deployment instructions and environment configuration. Modifications and releases of the items are controlled and made available to the customer and work team including the storage, handling and delivery of the items, with all documents stored in structured project directory, cloud backup (Google Drive/OneDrive) for safety and redundancy, version numbering convention (Section 5.1) for document versions, and access control and change tracking through Git history.

**PM.07 – Software Quality Assurance**

Software quality assurance is performed to provide assurance that work products and processes comply with the Project Plan and Requirements Specification, with quality assurance activities including code review (self-review and advisor feedback) for adherence to coding standards, consistent coding standards (naming conventions, formatting, documentation), test coverage verification (target: ≥70% for critical services), documentation review and approval process, traceability verification (requirements → design → tests) using Traceability Record, and compliance checking against ISO/IEC 29110 process requirements. Quality assurance records are maintained in Test Plan and Test Records (`BridgeU-TestPlan.md`), Traceability Record (`BridgeU-TraceabilityRecord.md`), code review notes and advisor feedback, and quality metrics and coverage reports. Quality issues and non-conformances are identified, tracked, and resolved before project closure.

