### 4.2 Software Implementation Process (SI)

#### 4.2.1 Purpose

The purpose of the Software Implementation Process is the systematic performance of the analysis, design, construction, integration and tests activities for new or modified software products according to the specified requirements.

#### 4.2.2 Software Implementation Activities

The following activities are performed in accordance with ISO/IEC 29110:

**SI.01 – Task Execution Through Project Plan**

Tasks of the activities are performed through the accomplishment of the current Project Plan, where all development, testing, and documentation tasks follow the detailed WBS defined in Section 7, including Phase 1 (Proposal development and defense), Phase 2 (SRS, SDD, Project Plan, and Feature #1 implementation), Phase 3 (Feature #2 and #3 implementation, comprehensive testing), and Phase 4 (Finalization, documentation, and closure). Progress is tracked against the Project Plan milestones and schedule, with task completion status updated regularly, milestone deliverables verified before proceeding to next phase, and deviations from plan identified and addressed through PM.02.

**SI.02 – Software Requirements Definition and Analysis**

Software requirements are defined, analyzed for correctness and testability, approved by the customer, baselined, and communicated, with requirements documented in `BridgeU-SRS.md` including functional requirements for all three features (Feature #1: Daily Briefing System with news crawling, AI summarization/translation, search/filter; Feature #2: Community Interaction Platform with posts, comments, likes, tags, AI moderation, AI comment summary, mutual follow list, private messaging; Feature #3: Authentication & Profile with registration, login/logout, profile management, "My Community Posts"), non-functional requirements (performance, security, usability, reliability), use case diagrams and detailed use case descriptions for each feature, and system constraints and assumptions. Requirements are analyzed for correctness (validated against user needs and project objectives from Proposal), testability (each requirement can be verified through testing), completeness (all features from Proposal are covered with no gaps), consistency (requirements do not conflict with each other), and clarity (requirements are unambiguous and understandable). Requirements are approved by the advisor (customer) through milestone reviews, with Progress I review including SRS approval, feedback incorporated and requirements refined, and requirements baselined after approval and communicated to all stakeholders. Changes to requirements follow the change management process (PM.03), with change requests evaluated for impact, approved changes updating SRS and Traceability Record, and requirements re-baselined after changes.

**SI.03 – Software Architectural and Detailed Design**

Software architectural and detailed design is developed and baselined, where design describes the software items and internal and external interfaces of them, and consistency and traceability to software requirements are established. Design is documented in `BridgeU-SDDv.2.md` including system architecture (logical architecture with layered architecture of Presentation, Business, Data layers; physical architecture with frontend Vue.js, backend Spring Boot, database MySQL, external services Qwen AI and Google News RSS), Data Flow Diagrams (DFD) at Level 0, 1, and 2 showing data flows for each feature, Entity-Relationship Diagram (ERD) showing all database entities and relationships, data dictionary for all database tables with field descriptions, types, and constraints, component design (Controller, Service, Repository layers) with responsibilities, API contracts (endpoints, request/response formats, status codes, error handling), and database schema design (tables, indexes, foreign keys, constraints). Design consistency is verified, ensuring all SRS requirements are addressed in the design, design components map to requirements via Traceability Record, internal interfaces (between components) are clearly defined, external interfaces (APIs, database, external services) are specified, and design is implementable with chosen technologies. Design is baselined after advisor review and approval, with Progress I review including SDD approval, and design refined based on feedback (e.g., SDD v1 → v2 with complete DFDs/ERD).

**SI.04 – Software Component Construction and Unit Testing**

Software components defined by the design are produced, where unit test is defined and performed to verify the consistency with requirements and the design, and traceability to the requirements and design are established. Component construction follows the layered architecture, including Entity Layer (data models such as User, Post, Comment, News, Message, Tag with JPA annotations), Repository Layer (data access using Spring Data JPA with custom query methods), Service Layer (business logic including NewsService, PostService, AuthService, MessageService), Controller Layer (REST API endpoints with request validation and response formatting), DTO Layer (data transfer objects for API requests/responses), Frontend Components (Vue.js components such as DailyBriefingList.vue, CommunityPage.vue, ProfilePage.vue), and Frontend Composables (reusable logic such as useAuth, useApi). Unit tests are written for service methods (business logic) using JUnit and Mockito, repository queries and data access methods, utility functions and helper classes, frontend components and composables using Vue Test Utils, and API endpoint handlers (controller methods). Unit tests verify that components behave according to design specifications, components meet their requirements from SRS, components handle error cases correctly (validation, exception handling), and components integrate correctly with dependencies (mocked). Test cases are documented in `BridgeU-TestPlan.md` with test case ID, description, preconditions, test steps and expected results, traceability to requirements and design components, and test execution results and coverage metrics.

**SI.05 – Software Integration and Integration Testing**

Software is produced performing integration of software components and verified using test cases and test procedures, where results are recorded at the test report, defects are corrected, and consistency and traceability to Software Design are established. Integration activities include Backend Integration (Controller → Service → Repository → Database, where REST endpoints call service methods, service methods use repository interfaces, and repository implementations interact with MySQL database), Frontend-Backend Integration (Vue components → Axios → REST APIs → Backend, where frontend components make HTTP requests via Axios, backend REST APIs process requests and return responses, and data flows correctly between frontend and backend), and External Service Integration (Qwen AI API for summarization and translation, email service for notifications, and Google News RSS for news crawling). Integration test cases are defined and executed, including API endpoint testing (using Postman or automated tests to test all CRUD operations, authentication and authorization, and error handling and edge cases), database integration testing (testing CRUD operations with real database, relationships and constraints, and transaction handling), external service integration testing (testing with mock responses for unit tests, real API for integration tests, and error handling for service failures), and end-to-end workflow testing (complete user workflows such as create post, add comment, like post, and cross-feature integration such as daily briefing → community discussion). Integration test results are recorded in `BridgeU-TestPlan.md` with test case ID, description, steps, expected results, actual results, pass/fail status, defects found and their resolution, and integration coverage metrics. Defects identified during integration testing are documented with description and steps to reproduce, fixed and verified through re-testing, and tracked until closure in defect log. Consistency with Software Design is verified, ensuring integrated components match design specifications from SDD, interfaces between components work as designed, data flows match DFDs, and traceability to design is maintained.

**SI.06 – Software Configuration Integration and Baseline**

A software configuration that meets the requirements specification as agreed to with the customer, which includes user, operation, and maintenance documentation, is integrated, baselined, and stored at the project repository, where needs for changes to the software configuration are detected and related change requests are initiated. Software configuration includes source code (complete frontend Vue.js and backend Spring Boot code), database schema (MySQL schema with Flyway migration scripts for version control), build scripts (Maven/Gradle build files, npm package.json with dependencies), deployment instructions (`DEPLOYMENT.md` with environment setup and deployment steps), user documentation (user guide if applicable, API documentation Swagger/OpenAPI), operation documentation (system administration guide, backup procedures, monitoring guidelines), and maintenance documentation (code comments, architecture documentation in SDD, troubleshooting guide). Software configuration is integrated, ensuring all components work together as a complete system, system meets all requirements specified in SRS, system is ready for system testing and deployment, and all dependencies are resolved and compatible. Software configuration is baselined, with final version tagged in Git repository (e.g., v1.0, v2.0), all documents finalized and versioned, configuration items listed and stored in project repository, and baseline documented with version numbers and dates. Change detection and requests involve identifying changes needed during testing or deployment, initiating change requests following PM.03 process, implementing approved changes and re-baselining configuration, and maintaining change history for audit purposes.

**SI.07 – Verification and Validation**

Verification and validation tasks of all required work products are performed using the defined criteria to achieve consistency among output and input products in each activity, where defects are identified and corrected, and records are stored in the verification/validation results. Verification activities (checking that work products meet specifications) include Requirements Verification (verifying SRS requirements are complete, consistent, and testable, ensuring all requirements from Proposal are addressed, requirements are unambiguous and measurable, and requirements can be traced to design and tests), Design Verification (verifying SDD design is consistent with requirements and implementable, ensuring design addresses all SRS requirements, design is traceable to requirements, and design is technically feasible with chosen technologies), Code Verification (verifying code follows design, coding standards, and requirements, ensuring code implements design specifications, code follows naming conventions and formatting standards, code includes appropriate comments and documentation, and code passes all unit and integration tests), and Test Verification (verifying test cases cover all requirements and design components, ensuring test cases are traceable to requirements, test cases cover all design components, and test coverage meets target of ≥70% for critical services). Validation activities (checking that work products meet user needs) include System Validation (system testing validates that the complete system meets user requirements, ensuring all features work as specified in use cases, system performs correctly under normal and edge conditions, and system meets non-functional requirements for performance, security, and usability), User Acceptance (advisor validates that system meets project objectives through final demo and presentation, advisor feedback and acceptance record, and system meeting expectations from Proposal), Functional Validation (all features work as specified in use cases, including Feature #1 Daily Briefing System, Feature #2 Community Interaction Platform, and Feature #3 Authentication & Profile), and Non-functional Validation (performance, security, and usability meet specified criteria, including response times meeting performance requirements, security measures such as authentication, authorization, and data protection in place, and user interface being intuitive and accessible). Verification/validation criteria include Requirements (all SRS requirements are addressed in design and tests), Design (design is traceable to requirements and implementable), Code (code coverage ≥ 70% for critical services, all tests pass), and System (all system test scenarios pass, no critical bugs remain). Verification/validation results are recorded in test execution records in `BridgeU-TestPlan.md` with pass/fail status, traceability verification in `BridgeU-TraceabilityRecord.md`, code review notes and advisor feedback, defect logs with resolution status, and coverage reports and quality metrics. Defects identified during verification/validation are documented with severity and priority, corrected and re-verified, tracked until closure, and records stored in test reports and defect logs.

